Good work — one refinement before I accept the change. Keep the scope strictly to `Set-Cookie` semantics.

Problem with the current branch B (`else if (preparedHeaders && !this.finalized)`): it also merges *non-cookie* prepared headers (`x-custom`, etc.) into raw handler responses. That changes long-standing behavior beyond this bug (prepared headers were deliberately only applied via `#newResponse()`), and the `!this.finalized` guard is a symptom of fighting that design. Narrow branch B so it only recovers prepared `Set-Cookie` values; all other prepared headers keep today's behavior (not merged).

Please restructure minimally and equivalently:

```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        // `set-cookie` is multi-value and is merged once, outside this loop
        if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
          continue
        }
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
      const resCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
      mergeSetCookieHeaders(_res.headers, this.#res.headers.getSetCookie(), resCookies)
    } else if (_res && this.#preparedHeaders && !this.finalized) {
      // `c.header()`/`setCookie()` calls made before a Response existed are kept
      // in `#preparedHeaders`. They may already have been copied into `_res` by
      // `c.newResponse()`, so only recover the cookies that are missing.
      const preparedCookies = this.#preparedHeaders.getSetCookie()
      if (preparedCookies.length) {
        _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
        const resCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
        const missingCookies = preparedCookies.filter((cookie) => !resCookies.includes(cookie))
        if (missingCookies.length) {
          mergeSetCookieHeaders(_res.headers, missingCookies, resCookies)
        }
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```

(Exact structure is yours; the requirements are: (1) branch A unchanged in behavior — cookie merge, chronological order, content-type skip, other headers old-wins; (2) branch B touches ONLY `set-cookie`, does not merge any other prepared header, avoids the Response clone when there is nothing to merge, keeps the `!this.finalized` guard; (3) `_res === undefined` unchanged.)

