## Finding 1 (BLOCKING): duplicate cookies when the new Response already carries the current cookies

`#newResponse()` builds its headers from `new Headers(this.#res.headers)` (src/context.ts ~line 634), so a response returned by `c.text()`/`c.json()`/`c.html()`/`c.body()` already contains the current `Set-Cookie` values. Branch 1 then concatenates `this.#res` cookies with `_res` cookies unconditionally, emitting each cookie twice. Verified failure scenario:

```js
app.use('*', async (c, next) => {
  await next()
  c.res = c.text('overwritten')   // derives headers from the existing c.res
})
app.get('/', (c) => {
  setCookie(c, 'handler', '1', { path: '/' })
  return c.text('hi')
})
// expected (and HEAD behavior): ['handler=1; Path=/']
// current change: ['handler=1; Path=/', 'handler=1; Path=/']
```

Also `c.res = c.res` now doubles every cookie (HEAD produced each once).

Required fix: make `mergeSetCookieHeaders` skip an `earlier` cookie when the `later` list already contains the exact same string, e.g.

```ts
const mergeSetCookieHeaders = (headers: Headers, earlier: string[], later: string[]) => {
  headers.delete('set-cookie')
  for (const cookie of earlier) {
    if (!later.includes(cookie)) {
      headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
    }
  }
  for (const cookie of later) {
    headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
  }
}
```

Keep internal duplicates that exist on only one side intact (only cross-side identical values are skipped), and keep the ordering contract (earlier/context cookies first, later/handler cookies last so a handler can override a same-named cookie).

## Finding 2 (MEDIUM): unnecessary Response clone in branch 2

In the `#preparedHeaders` branch the clone currently happens before deciding whether anything is missing, so the common `c.text()` + prepared cookie path clones on every request (losing the original Response identity/`url`/`type`). Compute `resCookies`/`missingCookies` from the original `_res` first and only `createResponseInstance(...)` when `missingCookies.length > 0`.

